'''HRX, S.A.''' (formerly '''Henri Rouche Empreses''') is a Balboan retail company and the country's largest department store operator. Headquartered in the [[El Comerç]] district of [[Barcelona (ciutat)|Barcelona]], [[Sant Cristòfor]], the company operates a chain of department stores, shopping centres, and convenience stores across the Balboan isthmus and, since 2024, in [[Tapuya]]. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker HRX.

HRX was founded in 1958 as a single variety store in [[Pandao]] by [[Henri Rouche]] (1922–1984), a Black Balboan entrepreneur. Under the management of Rouche's two sons, the company expanded from a single neighbourhood store into a national retail chain, becoming the first major Balboan retail enterprise founded and built by a Black Balboan family. The company rebranded from Henri Rouche Empreses to HRX in 2001, as part of a modernisation programme that accompanied its listing on the Bolsa.

The company operates three principal retail formats: the HRX department stores and shopping centres, the [[Botiga Ràpida]] convenience store chain (acquired 2018), and a portfolio of shopping centres and department stores in Tapuya (acquired 2024) and Sinchew (acquired 2025).

== History ==

=== The Rouche family ===

The Rouche family's history in Balboa traces to the era of slavery. Henri Rouche's grandfather, [[Auguste Rouche]], was enslaved on a plantation in the interior. Following his emancipation, he was assisted by a man named Rouche — whose full name and background are not known — who paid for his passage to [[Sant Cristòfor]]. In gratitude, Auguste adopted the name Rouche, a surname that was uncommon in Balboa and that has remained distinctive in the Balboan naming tradition.

Auguste Rouche settled in the Black Quarter of [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], the old city of Sant Cristòfor, where the freed Black Balboan community was concentrated in the post-emancipation period. The family lived in Original for a generation before Henri's father, [[Pau Rouche]], moved across the bay to the [[Pandao]] district on the [[Barcelona (ciutat)|Barcelona]] peninsula.

Pau Rouche was the first member of his family to attend university. He enrolled at the [[University of Sant Cristòfor]], the first institution of higher education in the [[Zong Balboa]] dependency to admit Black and white students together, which had been established in 1916 under the egalitarian reforms of Governor [[Lîm Bûn-cheng]]. Pau was among the first graduating classes of the integrated university, graduating with a degree in pharmacy in 1920. He moved to Pandao to establish a pharmacy and variety store serving the growing Black Balboan middle class of the Barcelona peninsula. The store offered patent medicines, toiletries, household goods, and basic groceries, and it became a community institution in the Pandao district.

The [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947) devastated the Zong Balboa dependency. The siege of Sant Cristòfor produced heavy bombing, famine, and widespread destruction, and the Pandao district — a working-class area on the peninsula — was severely damaged. Pau Rouche's pharmacy and variety store were destroyed during the siege, and the family was displaced. Pau survived the war but was left with limited resources to rebuild.

=== Henri Rouche (1922–1984) ===

[[Henri Rouche]] was born in 1922 in Pandao. He served in the [[Zong Balboa]] army during the Great Adonian War, defending the besieged dependency through the later years of the conflict. After the war and the dissolution of the Zong Balboa dependency in 1950, Henri worked as a pharmacy assistant to his father, who had re-established a modest pharmacy in Pandao during the post-war reconstruction.

Pau Rouche died in 1958, and Henri, inheriting his father's pharmacy and a small savings, opened his own store — a variety store offering household goods, clothing, and basic groceries — in Pandao. The store, which traded as Henri Rouche Empreses, was located on Avinguda del Port and served the Pandao district's working-class community. Rouche's business model was simple: buy in bulk, keep margins low, and serve the community that he knew.

The expansion under Henri was modest. He opened a second location in the neighbouring El Fort district in 1962, and a third in the Dàrsena district in 1970. By the time of his death in 1984, the company operated five stores, all in the Barcelona peninsula and the adjacent districts of Sant Cristòfor. The company was profitable but small, and its growth had been limited by Henri's conservative approach and his focus on the community that he knew.

=== Expansion under the sons (1984–2001) ===

Henri Rouche died in 1984, and the company was inherited by his two sons, [[Marc Rouche i Masdeu]] and [[David Rouche i Masdeu]], who had been working in the business since their teens. The brothers, who had benefited from the educational opportunities of the post-reunification era — both were graduates of the [[Sant Cristòfor Academy]] — brought a more ambitious vision to the company.

The brothers accelerated the expansion, opening stores across the Sant Cristòfor metropolitan area and, in the 1990s, extending into the interior cities. The expansion was driven by a strategy of locating stores in underserved communities — the working-class neighbourhoods of the Estret corridor, the interior cities that lacked modern retail, and the Birú communities that were ignored by the Volisanian-owned retail chains. The strategy was both commercial and personal: the brothers, who had grown up in a Black Balboan working-class community, understood the purchasing power and the retail needs of communities that the established retail sector had neglected.

By 2000, Henri Rouche Empreses operated 24 department stores across the isthmus and had become the largest Balboan-owned retail chain. The company's success — built by a Black Balboan family from a working-class background — was celebrated as a symbol of the post-reunification economic transformation, and the Rouche brothers became prominent figures in the Balboan business community.

=== Rebranding and listing (2001) ===

In 2001, the company rebranded from Henri Rouche Empreses to HRX, a modernisation that was intended to signal the company's transition from a family business to a national retail brand. The rebranding was accompanied by the company's listing on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]], with the Rouche family retaining a 35 per cent stake. The listing raised capital that was used to fund the development of the HRX shopping centre format — large-format retail complexes that combined department stores, specialty retail, food courts, and entertainment.

The first HRX shopping centre — the '''HRX Gran Centre Comercial''' — opened in 2003 in Pandao, built over the modernised [[Pandao Ferry Terminal]] and only a few blocks from the site of Henri Rouche's original store. The Gran Centre Comercial became a flagship for the format and a symbol of the district's transformation from a working-class neighbourhood to a mixed-use urban community.

=== Botiga Ràpida acquisition (2018) ===

In 2018, HRX acquired [[Botiga Ràpida]], a chain of 180 convenience stores operating in the Estret corridor and the interior cities. The acquisition was a significant departure from the company's department store and shopping centre business, and it was driven by the brothers' recognition that the convenience store format — small-format, neighbourhood-anchored, focused on everyday essentials — was the natural extension of the community retail model that had defined the company since its founding.

The Botiga Ràpida chain was rebranded under the HRX umbrella but retained its distinct format and brand identity. The convenience stores operate as a separate division within HRX, with their own management and merchandising strategy, but they share the company's supply chain, logistics, and technology infrastructure.

=== Tapuya expansion (2024) ===

In 2024, HRX acquired the [[Grup de Centres Comercials de Tapuya]] (GCCT), a Tapuyan shopping centre and department store operator, in a transaction that represented the company's first international expansion. The acquisition added five shopping centres and 20 department stores to the HRX portfolio, establishing the company as a significant retail operator in the Tapuyan market.

The GCCT acquisition was driven by the recognition that the Balboan retail market was approaching saturation, and that the Tapuyan market — which was less developed in modern retail infrastructure and had a growing urban middle class — offered the same opportunity that the Balboan interior had offered in the 1990s. The Rouche brothers cited the family's history of serving underserved communities as the strategic rationale for the acquisition.

The Tapuyan operations were integrated into the HRX corporate structure as a separate international division, with local management retained and the stores rebranded under the HRX banner.

=== Sinchew expansion (2025) ===

In 2025, HRX acquired the '''大豐商場集團''' ({{lang|zh|Tuā-hong Tiong-tiûⁿ Chi̍p-thuân}}; "Great Abundance Mall Group"), a [[Sinchew]]-based shopping centre operator, in a transaction that gave HRX access to 18 shopping centres across the Sinchew market. The acquisition represented the company's second international expansion, following the GCCT acquisition in Tapuya.

Unlike the Tapuya acquisition, which had targeted an underdeveloped retail market, the Sinchew acquisition was a turnaround play. Sinchew is an upper-middle-income country with a mature retail sector, and 大豐商場集團 was the country's third-largest mall operator — a struggling business that had been losing market share to the two dominant Sinchew retail groups for several years. The company's 18 shopping centres were well-located in the principal Sinchew urban centres, but the operator had suffered from underinvestment, poor tenant management, and a lack of modern retail technology.

HRX acquired the group at a significant discount, citing the opportunity to apply its community-anchored retail model and its modernised supply chain and technology infrastructure to a portfolio of well-located but underperforming properties. The Rouche brothers noted that the Sinchew acquisition, combined with the Tapuya portfolio, positioned HRX as a multi-national retail operator with a presence across three countries.

The Sinchew operations were integrated as a separate international division, with local management retained. The shopping centres are being progressively renovated and rebranded under the HRX banner, with the first rebranded centres expected to reopen in 2027.

=== Modern era (2018–present) ===

The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 had a mixed impact on HRX. The department stores and shopping centres were forced to close during the lockdowns, producing significant revenue losses, but the Botiga Ràpida convenience stores were classified as essential and remained operational, providing a partial offset. The company accelerated its e-commerce investment during the pandemic, developing an online ordering and delivery platform for both the department stores and the convenience stores.

By 2026, HRX operated 58 department stores (38 in Balboa, 20 in Tapuya), 35 shopping centres (12 in Balboa, 5 in Tapuya, 18 in Sinchew), and 220 Botiga Ràpida convenience stores, and employed approximately 35,000 people. The Rouche family retained a 28 per cent stake, and the company remained the largest Balboan-owned retail enterprise.

== Operations ==

HRX operates three principal retail divisions:

* '''HRX department stores''': The company's original format, operating 58 stores across Balboa and Tapuya. The department stores offer clothing, household goods, electronics, cosmetics, and groceries, with a focus on affordable pricing and community-anchored locations. In Balboa, the stores are concentrated in the Estret corridor (18), the interior cities (14), and the Birú communities (6). In Tapuya, the 20 stores acquired from GCCT are concentrated in the principal urban centres.
* '''HRX shopping centres''': Large-format retail complexes that combine an anchor HRX department store with specialty retail, food courts, and entertainment. The company operates 17 shopping centres — 12 in Balboa and 5 in Tapuya — primarily in the Estret corridor, the larger interior cities, and the Tapuyan urban centres.
* '''Botiga Ràpida''': The convenience store chain, operating 220 small-format stores in the Estret corridor and the interior cities. The stores offer everyday essentials, groceries, and prepared food, with a focus on neighbourhood-anchored locations and extended hours.

The company's headquarters are at 1 Plaça d'El Comerç, in the El Comerç district of Barcelona, Sant Cristòfor.

